also be a component, I should think about how to structure. But minimal: check if all items are runes and length === 3, then 60.\n\nWait, what about mixed? 3 runes + 0 moonstones = block. 3 runes + 1 moonstone = not a pure block, but 3 runes should still be a block? The spec says \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" and \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\". So blocks are formed within each type. 3 runes + 1 moonstone would be 60 + 25 = 85 base.\n\nSo I need to group by component type. For each component type, count items. For each group of exactly 3, base = 60. For leftovers, base = 25 each.\n\nMinimal for runes only: count runes, form blocks of 3.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Red Phase Complete:\n**Compilation Prediction**: None expected Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected { results: [{ premium: 71 }] }, received { results: [{ premium: 88 }] } Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.</think>## Green -- Test 6: three-rune block discount\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:51","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":645,"output":405,"cacheRead":33664,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":314,"totalTokens":34714,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784925264515,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-ead20ee2-d2b8-4c46-be0e-3b6392f8acc4","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:51","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n\ntype QuoteResult = { premium: number };\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: unknown[] };\n\nconst itemBasePremium = (item: unknown): number => {\n  const itemWithType = item as { type: string };\n  if (itemWithType.type === \"amulet\") return 60;\n  if (itemWithType.type === \"rune\") return 25;\n  return 100;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: unknown[]): number => {\n  const policyBase = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemBasePremium(item), 0);\n  const beforeFee = policyBase * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE);\n  const roundedToNearestCent = Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100;\n  return Math.ceil(roundedToNearestCent + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (input: { steps: unknown[] }): { results: QuoteResult[] } => {\n  const results: QuoteResult[] = [];\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    const quoteStep = step as QuoteStep;\n    if (quoteStep.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({ premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items) });\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784925267197},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Need to implement block discount. Minimal: for exactly 3 runes, base = 60. But the block logic should be more general. Since only runes exist so far, I'll implement grouping by component type with blocks.\n\nLet me add a helper for component base premium. Actually, I can modify itemBasePremium to not apply to components, and add a component premium calculator. But minimal change: in calculateQuotePremium, if all items are runes and count === 3, use 60.\n\nHmm, but next test is 4 runes → no block. So with minimal approach, I need to handle counts other than 3. Let me implement grouping properly from the start for components.\n\nI'll change itemBasePremium to return 0 for components and add a component pricing function. But that's bigger. Alternatively, keep itemBasePremium returning 25 for runes, and in calculateQuotePremium detect blocks.\n\nActually the simplest minimal change: after computing policyBase normally, check if exactly 3 runes and replace policyBase with 60. But then 4 runes won't use block