rovements:\n1. Reduce type-cast duplication with an `itemType` helper.\n2. Rename `itemBasePremium` to `nonComponentBasePremium` to better reflect it excludes component types.\n\nWait - naming is first priority. Does `itemBasePremium` clearly describe what it does? It returns base premium for items that are not components (amulet, sword). The name is too generic. A better name would be `nonComponentBasePremium` or `mainItemBasePremium`.\n\nDoes `componentBasePremium` clearly describe? It calculates base premium for a count of components. Actually, since it only handles the case where all components are alike (block discount), maybe `alikeComponentBasePremium`? But current tests only cover runes, and the function is generic by count. It might be premature to rename to rune-specific.\n\nLet me think about APP mass calculations.\n\nCurrent code mass:\n```\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\n\ntype QuoteResult = { premium: number };\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: unknown[] };\n\nconst itemBasePremium = (item: unknown): number => {\n  const itemWithType = item as { type: string };\n  if (itemWithType.type === \"amulet\") return 60;\n  if (itemWithType.type === \"rune\") return 25;\n  return 100;\n};\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM = 25;\n\nconst componentBasePremium = (count: number): number => {\n  if (count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE) return COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM;\n  return count * COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM;\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (items: unknown[]): number => {\n  const mainItems = items.filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes((item as { type: string }).type));\n  const mainBase = mainItems.reduce((sum, item) => sum + itemBasePremium(item), 0);\n  const runeCount = items.filter((item) => (item as { type: string }).type === \"rune\").length;\n  const policyBase = mainBase + componentBasePremium(runeCount);\n  const beforeFee = policyBase * (1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE);\n  const roundedToNearestCent = Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100;\n  return Math.ceil(roundedToNearestCent + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n```\n\nMass breakdown:\nConstants: 5 (5, 0.1, \"rune\", \"moonstone\", 3, 60, 25) = 5\nActually string literals count too? Let me re-read APP: Constant (Mass: 1): Literal values (5, \"hello\", true). So \"rune\" and \"moonstone\" are constants. \"amulet\" is constant. Total constants: 5 (numeric) + 3 (string: \"amulet\", \"rune\" twice? Actually \"rune\" appears in component types and in filter. Literal \"rune\" appears 2 times, \"moonstone\" 1 time, \"amulet\" 1 time. But as values they each count. Hmm, mass is per occurrence? Yes I think per occurrence in the code. Let me count carefully.\n\nActually I should count each literal value occurrence:\n- 5 (PROCESSING_FEE) = 1\n- 0.1 (FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE) = 1\n- \"amulet\" = 1\n- \"rune\" in itemBasePremium = 1\n- \"rune\" in COMPONENT_TYPES = 1\n- \"moonstone\" = 1\n- 3 = 1\n- 60 = 1\n- 25 = 1\n- 1 in (1 + ...) = 1\n- 100 in Math.round(...*100) = 1\n- 100 in itemBasePremium return = 1\n\nConstants total: 12\n\nBindings:\n- itemBasePremium: item, itemWithType = 2\n- componentBasePremium: count = 1\n- calculateQuotePremium: items, mainItems, mainBase, runeCount, policyBase, beforeFee, roundedToNearestCent = 7\n- processScenario: input, results, step, quoteStep = 4\n- reduce callback: sum, item = 2\n- filter callback item (x2) = 2\nTotal bindings: 18\n\nInvocations:\n- item as {type: string}: 3 occurrences = 3 (cast is invocation-like? In APP, type cast isn't explicitly mentioned. I think we ignore casts or count them as invocations? The APP list says \"Invocation: Function calls\". A cast is not a function call. I'll ignore casts.)\n- itemWithType.type === \"amulet\": property access? APP doesn't count property access separately.\n- includes: 1\n- reduce: 1\n- itemBasePremium(item): 1\n- fil