earestCent`? It explains the rounding step. Removing it would hurt clarity. Keep it.\n\nDo we need `beforeFee`? It explains the surcharge step. Keep.\n\nDo we need `policyBase`? It explains the base before surcharge. Keep.\n\nDo we need `mainBase`? Keep.\n\nThese explaining variables improve Rule 2.\n\nOne more potential duplication: `items.filter(...)` is used twice. We could extract a count function. But `filter` + `length` is already concise. A helper `countItemsByType` might add more elements than it removes. Let me check.\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst mainItems = items.filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(itemType(item)));\nconst runeCount = items.filter((item) => itemType(item) === \"rune\").length;\n```\n\nIf I extract:\n```typescript\nconst countByType = (items: unknown[], type: string): number =>\n  items.filter((item) => itemType(item) === type).length;\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nconst mainItems = items.filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(itemType(item)));\nconst runeCount = countByType(items, \"rune\");\n```\n\nThis reduces duplication of the filter pattern but adds a function. Mass-wise, it might be similar or higher. Clarity-wise, it's reasonable. But for Rule 4 (fewest elements), adding a function for one use might not be worth it. Let me keep as is for now.\n\nActually, the filter pattern is duplicated conceptually (we filter items by type), but the actual predicates are different. Not strong duplication.\n\nLet me reconsider `itemType` helper extraction. Currently we cast 3 times:\n1. In `nonComponentBasePremium`\n2. In `mainItems` filter\n3. In `runeCount` filter\n\nExtracting `itemType` removes 2 casts (the third becomes `itemType(item)`). It adds one function. Good for Rule 3. It also improves Rule 2 by naming the operation.\n\nNow let me write the refactored file and run tests.\n\nMass calculation for refactored code:\n\nConstants: Same 12.\n\nBindings:\n- itemType: item = 1\n- nonComponentBasePremium: item, type = 2\n- compon