d (1)\n      Tests  1 failed | 10 passed | 22 todo (33)\n   Start at  20:36:31\n   Duration  161ms (transform 28ms, setup 0ms, collect 26ms, tests 8ms, environment 0ms, prepare 43ms)\n\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n\n\nCommand exited with code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784925391681},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Red complete. Green: add curse surcharge. Need to apply item-specific modifiers to per-item base premium.\n\nCurrently nonComponentBasePremium just returns base. I need to apply cursed +50% and enchantment +30% to each item's base. For cursed sword: 100 * 1.5 = 150. Then first insurance 10% of 150 = 15. Fee 5. Total 150 + 15 + 5 = 170? Wait that gives 170, not 165.\n\nWait, let me recalculate using the spec formula. The integration example: \"100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G\". So curse is 50% of base = 50, added to base = 150. First insurance is 10% of policy base = 10% of 150 = 15. Then fee 5. 150 + 15 + 5 = 170. But spec says 165. \n\nWait, the formula shown is: 100 + 50 + 10 = 160 + 5 = 165. So first insurance is 10% of base (100) = 10, not 10% of 150. That means item-specific modifiers are applied after policy-wide modifiers? No, the example says policy-wide modifiers apply to policy base (sum of item base premiums). But in the example, the policy base for first insurance seems to be the base before curse surcharge.\n\nHmm, re-reading the modifier scope example:\n\"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\"\n\nSo policy base premium = sum of item base premiums = 100 + 60 = 160. Then cursed surcharge = 50% of cursed sword's base = 50. Total before policy-wide modifiers = 160 + 50 = 210. Then loyalty/first insurance/follow-up