ecide.\n\n5. Rename `basePremiumForStandardItem` removed. But `basePremiumForItemType` remains.\n\nNeed ensure type compatibility. The `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` currently called in standardItemTotals with `item` typed unknown. If standardItemTotals filters unknown items, we need cast before calling. We can map/cast.\n\nAlternative: Keep `standardItemTotals` accepting unknown[] and cast inside. But then no centralization. Better to cast at top of calculateQuotePremium.\n\nLet's write new code.\n\nOriginal types:\n```\ntype QuoteResult = { premium: number };\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: unknown[] };\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number };\n```\n\nAdd maybe `type Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };`. But not necessary; adds element.\n\nRefactored functions:\n```\nconst basePremiumFor = (item: Item): number => basePremiumForItemType(item.type);\n\nconst surchargeForItem = (item: Item): number => {\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForItemType(item.type);\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed ? basePremium * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentSurcharge =\n    (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? basePremium * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n```\nRename `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` to `surchargeForItem` maybe clearer. Need update call in standardItemTotals.\n\n```\nconst standardItemTotals = (items: Item[]): { base: number; surcharges: number } =>\n  items\n    .filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type))\n    .reduce(\n      (totals, item) => ({\n        base: totals.base + basePremiumFor(item),\n        surcharges: totals.surcharges + surchargeForItem(item),\n      }),\n      { base: 0, surcharges: 0 },\n    );\n```\n\n```\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: unknown[],\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const typedItems = items.map((item) => item as Item);\n  const { base: standardBase, surcharges: itemSurcharges } = standardItemTotals(typedItems);\n  const componentBase = COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce((sum, type) => {\n    const count = typedItems.filter((item) => item.type === type).length;\n    return sum + componentPremiumFor(count);\n  }, 0);\n  const policyBase = standardBase + componentBase;\n  return calculateFinalPremium(policyBase, itemSurcharges, customer.yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n};\n```\n\n`processScenario` unchanged.\n\nMass calculation for changed section:\nBefore: include itemType, basePremiumForStandardItem, itemSpecificSurchargeFor, standardItemTotals, calculateQuotePremium.\nAfter: include basePremiumFor, surchargeForItem, standardItemTotals, calculateQuotePremium. Plus maybe typedItems mapping.\n\nLet's compute APP mass in detail.\n\nAPP mass definitions:\n- Constant: literal values\n- Binding/Scalar: variables, parameters\n- Invocation: function calls (including method calls, operators? Wait \"+\", `>=`, `??`? The example counts `+` as invocation? In the example, they counted `reduce` and `+` as invocations. `+` is invocation? They said invocations: 2 (reduce, +). So arithmetic operators count as invocations? Hmm. They counted reduce (function call) and + (operator) as invocations. So binary operators likely count as invocations. In the example code `numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0)` had reduce and +. They count both. So `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `>=`, `===`, `?.`? Maybe boolean and comparison operators count as invocations. Ternary operator counted as conditional. `??`? Could be invocation? Not sure. But we can be consistent: count each binary operator and function call as invocation. The unary `!` maybe not. Cast `(item as Item)` is not invocation; `as` is type assertion. `item.type` property access maybe not invocation? In example they didn't count property access. We can ignore property access as invocation. Method call `.filter`, `.reduce`, `.map`, `.includes`, `.push`, `.ceil`, `.round` 