ificSurchargeFor`? `basePremiumForStandardItem`? Let's evaluate based on function purpose and all tests.\n\n- `calculateFinalPremium`: It applies policy-wide modifiers (first insurance surcharge, loyalty discount, follow-up discount), adds item surcharges, rounds, and adds processing fee. The name `calculateFinalPremium` is generic; maybe `finalizePremium` or `applyPolicyModifiers`? But it also adds fee. Since tests for first insurance, loyalty, follow-up are about policy-wide modifiers, name could be `applyPolicyWideModifiers`. But it also adds processing fee. `calculateFinalPremium` is okay.\n\n- `itemSpecificSurchargeFor`: Based on tests, it applies curse and high-enchantment surcharges per item. A clearer name might be `itemSurcharge` or `surchargesFor`. But `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` conveys item-specific modifiers. Could be okay.\n\n- `basePremiumForStandardItem`: It calculates base premium for a non-component item. With recent follow-up discount, nothing changed about this. Name is a bit long and \"standard item\" might be unclear. Could rename to `basePremiumForItem` to align with `basePremiumForItemType` (premium for one item). But `basePremiumForItemType` exists. Hmm.\n\n- `componentPremiumFor`: calculates component premium based on count. Good.\n\n- `standardItemTotals`: aggregates base and surcharges for non-component items. Good.\n\n- `calculateQuotePremium`: calculates premium for one quote. Good.\n\n- `processScenario`: processes scenario steps. Good.\n\nMaybe the biggest naming issue is `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` plural? It returns sum of surcharges, maybe `itemSurcharges` as noun. But the function is a helper; rename to `surchargesFor`? Let's decide to rename `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` to `surchargesForItem` and `basePremiumForStandardItem` to `basePremiumForItem`. That improves consistency: `basePremiumForItem` / `surchargesForItem`. This is a pure rename, no mass change. We can also remove redundant `itemType` helper? If rename only, keep code same.\n\nBut the instructions encourage at least one refactoring; rename is a refactoring. Evaluate naming first, then implement rename. Calculate mass before/after (same). Tests green.\n\nHowever, is there any duplication we can fix without increasing mass? The cast `item as Item` appears in `itemType` and `itemSpecificSurchargeFor`. We could remove `itemType` and inline `(item as Item).type` in `basePremiumForStandardItem` and filters, but that increases casts. Not good.\n\nWe could merge `basePremiumForStandardItem` and `itemSpecificSurchargeFor`? Not.\n\nMaybe a small DRY improvement: Both `basePremiumForStandardItem` and `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` compute `basePremiumForItemType(...type)`. We could have `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` call `basePremiumForStandardItem(item)` for basePremium. That removes one invocation of `basePremiumForItemType`, reducing duplication and mass. Let's examine:\nCurrent:\n```\nconst basePremiumForStandardItem = (item: unknown): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemType(itemType(item));\n\nconst itemSpecificSurchargeFor = (item: unknown): number => {\n  const typedItem = item as Item;\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForItemType(typedItem.type);\n  ...\n```\nWe can change itemSpecificSurchargeFor to:\n```\nconst basePremium = basePremiumForStandardItem(item);\n```\nBut `basePremiumForStandardItem` calls `itemType(item)` which casts. Then `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` no longer needs typedItem for basePremium, but still needs typedItem for cursed/enchantment. It can use `item as Item` once. That removes one `basePremiumForItemType` invocation and one property access, but adds an invocation to `basePremiumForStandardItem`. Net invocations: remove one basePremiumForItemType, add basePremiumForStandardItem (which internally calls itemType and basePremiumForItemType). Actually total invocations increase because now basePremiumForStandardItem is called. But conceptually reduces knowledge duplication of base premium lookup. Not huge.\n\nMaybe better to define `basePremiumForItemType` only and reuse.\n\nAnother DRY: `componentPremiumFor` and standard item premium both involve base premium. Not.\n\nLet's not overthink. We can do a combined refactoring:\n- Rename `basePremiumForStandardItem` → `basePremiumForItem`.\n- Rename `itemSpecificSurchargeFor` → `surchargesForItem`.\n- In `surchargesForItem`, reuse `basePremiumForItem(item)` instead of recomputing via `basePremiumForItemType`, reducing duplicated base-premium lookup. This is a small DRY improvement. It keeps mass roughly same. Tests green.\n\nBut wait `surchargesForItem` currently casts `item as Item`. It can cast once and use `basePremiumForItem(item)` which accepts unknown and does its own cast via itemType. That still double casts. To reduce casts, we could change `basePremiumForItem` to accept `Item` and do cast once at call sites. But call site `standardItemTotals` has unknown items. We can cast there. Hmm.\n\nMaybe a better refactor: change helpers to accept `Item`, and in `standardItemTotals` map cast. But we saw mass increased because of map. But if we also use this to reduce repeated base premium calculation and remove itemType, maybe acceptable.\n\nLet's consider alternative with mass reduction: We can remove `itemType` and `basePremiumForStandardItem` entirely, and centralize cast in `calculateQuotePremium`. That removes two functions, but adds map. Net mass slightly increased due to extra loop. But fewer functions and duplication. However Rule 4 fewest elements would favor removing functions; Rule 3 DRY remove duplicate casts. Rule 2 clarity improved (helpers operate on typed items). The slight mass increase is acceptable due to Rule 2. We can document.\n\nBut maybe we can avoid extra map by having `standardItemTotals` accept unknown[] and cast inside, and component base also cast inside. That doesn't centralize but still removes `itemType` and `basePremiumForStandardItem`. Mass might reduce.\n\nLet's design a cleaner refactor that lowers mass:\n- 