tems.reduce(\n  (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType((item as Item).type),\n  0,\n);\n```\n\nThis is similar to other item value summations. Could extract to `totalInsuranceValue(items)`. But it's only used once. Maybe not worth it. But it would reveal intent.\n\nActually, looking at this, I can compute insuranceSum during the single-pass quote calculation if I'm doing that. Wait, insuranceSum is the sum of insuranceValueForItemType for all items. In the single-pass approach for quote premium, I'm already iterating items. I could also accumulate insuranceSum there. But that mixes concerns - quote premium and policy cap. Hmm, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, I can write a generic helper for summing by item type. But then we'd have more abstractions.\n\n**Refactoring E: Use discriminated union for Step**\n\nThe types are:\n```typescript\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: unknown[] };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: unknown };\n```\n\nWe can define:\n```typescript\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n```\n\nAnd cast `step as Step`. This is a small naming improvement.\n\n**Refactoring F: Improve processClaim**\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: unknown): ClaimResult => {\n  const typedIncident = incident as { damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[] };\n  let payout = 0;\n  for (const damage of typedIncident.damages) {\n    payout += damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  payout = Math.min(payout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n\nCould use reduce:\n```typescript\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: unknown): ClaimResult => {\n  const typedIncident = incident as { damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[] };\n  const grossPayout = typedIncident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE, 0);\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n\nThis removes the for loop and assignments, but adds reduce loop and constants. Net:\n- Remove: 1 for loop (5), 2 assignments (12), binding damage (1)\n- Add: 1 reduce loop (5), constants (1), bindings sum, damage, grossPayout (3), invocations reduce (2)\nNet mass change: -5 -12 -1 +5 +1 +3 +2 = -7\n\nAlso improves clarity by removing mutable `payout`. This is a good refactor.\n\nBut wait, `let payout = 0` - is the initializer an assignment? I said no. `payout += ...` is an assignment. `payout = Math.min(...)` is an assignment. So removing those removes 2 assignments (12 mass). Good.\n\nActually, using reduce for this might be less readable for some, but it's a standard functional pattern. The name `grossPayout` reveals intent.\n\n**Refactoring G: Single-pass scenario processing?**\n\nIn `processScenario`, we process steps and for each quote step, we call `calculateQuotePremium` and compute insuranceSum. If we move insuranceSum calculation into `calculateQuotePremium`, we could return both premium and insuranceSum. But that changes the function's responsibility. Hmm.\n\nActually, `calculateQuotePremium` returns a number. We could change it to return `{ premium: number; insuranceValue: number }`. But that affects naming. Maybe `quotePolicy` would be better? This is a bigger change.\n\nAlternatively, we can compute insuranceSum with a single reduce, which is fine.\n\nLet me prioritize:\n1. Naming evaluation - I'll document.\n2. Apply Rule 2 (Reveal Intent) - improve type casting, use discriminated union, add Step type, maybe rename isFirstQuote? No, isFirstQuote is okay.\n3. Apply Rule 3 (No Duplication) - single-pass quote calculation to avoid iterating items multiple times.\n4. Apply Rule 4 (Fewest Elements) - consider removing `standardItemTotals` if inlined.\n\nWait, I should be careful. Let me think about the single-pass quote calculation more. If I inline `standardItemTotals` into `calculateQuotePremium`, I remove a function (fewer elements), but I also remove the descriptive name. The body of `calculateQuotePremium` becomes longer. Is this a win for Rule 2? Maybe not. But if I use clear variable names and structure, it could be fine.\n\nActually, a better approach: Keep helper functions but make them more focused. The duplication is iterating over items multiple times. We can have one helper that returns `{ standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts }` in a single pass. Then `calculateQuotePremium` uses that. And `processScenario` can use the same helper for insurance sum? No, insurance sum uses a different value.\n\nHmm. Let me design:\n\n```typescript\ntype ItemPremiumBreakdown = {\n  standardBase: number;\n  itemSurcharges: number;\n  componentCounts: Record<string, number>;\n};\n\nconst itemPremiumBreakdown = (items: unknown[]): ItemPremiumBreakdown => {\n  let standardBase = 0;\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    const typedItem = item as Item;\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(typedItem.type)) {\n      componentCounts[typedItem.type] = (componentCounts[typedItem.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      standardBase += basePremiumForItem(item);\n      itemSurcharges += surchargesForItem(item);\n    }\n  }\n  return { standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts };\n};\n```\n\nThen `calculateQuotePremium`:\n```typescript\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: unknown[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const { standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts } = itemPremiumBreakdown(items);\n  const componentBase = COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce((sum, type) => {\n    return sum + componentPremiumFor(componentCounts[type] ?? 0);\n  }, 0);\n  const policyBase = standardBase + componentBase;\n  return calculateFinalPremium(policyBase, itemSurcharges, customer.yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n};\n```\n\nThis replaces `standardItemTotals` with `itemPremiumBreakdown`. The new helper has a clear name and does a single pass. But it adds a type alias `ItemPremiumBreakdown`. Is that worth it? It improves clarity.\n\nMass comparison for quote calculation area:\nBefore:\n- standardItemTotals: loops 2, invocations 5, bindings 3, constants 2\n- calculateQuotePremium: loops 2, invocations 5, bindings 10, constants 1\nTotal: loops 4, invocations 10, bindings 13, constants 3\n\nAfter:\n- itemPremiumBreakdown: loop 1, invocations 2 (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem - wait 3), bindings (items, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, item, typedItem = 6), assignments (standardBase = 0, itemSurcharges = 0, componentCounts[...] = ..., standardBase +=, itemSurcharges += = 5? but let initializers aren't assignments), conditionals (1 if), constants (0,0,0 = 3)\n- calculateQuotePremium: loop 1, invocations 3 (itemPremiumBreakdown, reduce, componentPremiumFor, calculateFinalPremium = 4), bindings (items, customer, isFirstQuote, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, policyBase, sum, type = 9), constants 1\n- type alias ItemPremiumBreakdown: not counted in mass? It's a type declaration.\n\nWait, I need to count assignments in itemPremiumBreakdown:\n- `let standardBase = 0;` - binding + constant, not assignment\n- `let itemSurcharges = 0;` - binding + constant, not assignment\n- `const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};` - binding + constant, not assignment\n- `componentCounts[typedItem.type] = (componentCounts[typedItem.type] ?? 0) + 1;` - assignment = (1)\n- `standardBase += basePremiumForItem(item);` - assignment += (1)\n- `itemSurcharges += surchargesForItem(item);` - assignment += (1)\nSo 3 assignments.\n\nMass after for quote area:\nitemPremiumBreakdown:\n- loops: 1 = 5\n- invocations: COMPONENT_TYPES.includes, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem = 3 = 6\n- bindings: items, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, item, typedItem = 6\n- constants: 0, 0, 0, 1 (the +1) = 4\n- conditionals: 1 = 4\n- assignments: 3 = 18\nTotal: 5+6+6+4+4+18 = 43\n\ncalculateQuotePremium:\n- loops: 1 = 5\n- invocations: itemPremiumBreakdown, reduce, componentPremiumFor, calculateFinalPremium = 4 = 8\n- bindings: items, customer, isFirstQuote, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, policyBase, sum, type = 9\n- constants: 0 = 1\nTotal: 5+8+9+1 = 23\n\nCombined after: 43 + 23 = 66\n\nBefore quote area:\nstandardItemTotals:\n- loops: 2 = 10\n- invocations: filter, includes, reduce, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem = 5 = 10\n- bindings: items, item, totals = 3\n- constants: 0, 0 = 2\nTotal: 10+10+3+2 = 25\n\ncalculateQuotePremium:\n- loops: 2 = 10\n- invocations: standardItemTotals, reduce, filter, componentPremiumFor, calculateFinalPremium = 5 = 10\n- bindings: items, customer, isFirstQuote, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentBase, policyBase, sum, type, count = 10\n- constants: 0 = 1\nTotal: 10+10+10+1 = 31\n\nCombined before: 25 + 31 = 56\n\nSo the new approach has higher mass (66 vs 56) because of assignments. But it has fewer loops (2 vs 4) which is conceptually simpler. Hmm, mass is higher. But Rule 2 (clarity) and Rule 3 (no duplication) might favor it.\n\nWait, but APP says lower mass is generally better. However, Rule 2 trumps APP. Does the single-pass approach reveal intent better? It removes the hidden double-iteration in standardItemTotals and the nested filtering in component calculation. It makes the algorithm more explicit. I think it's clearer overall, but it adds mutable state.\n\nAlternatively, I could use reduce for the single pass to avoid assignments:\n\n```typescript\nconst itemPremiumBreakdown = (items: unknown[]): ItemPremiumBreakdown =>\n  items.reduce((breakdown, item) => {\n    const typedItem = item as Item;\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(typedItem.type)) {\n      return {\n        ...breakdown,\n        componentCounts: {\n          ...breakdown.componentCounts,\n          [typedItem.type]: (breakdown.componentCounts[typedItem.type] ?? 0) + 1,\n        },\n      };\n    }\n    return {\n      ...breakdown,\n      standardBase: breakdown.standardBase + basePremiumForItem(item),\n      itemSurcharges: breakdown.itemSurcharges + surchargesForItem(item),\n    };\n  }, { standardBase: 0, itemSurcharges: 0, componentCounts: {} });\n```\n\nThis uses reduce (1 loop) but creates new objects each time. It avoids assignments but has more invocations/object creations. Actually, object spread `...` is not an invocation. It's an operator. But it creates objects. The instructions don't count object creation explicitly. Hmm.\n\nMass with reduce approach:\n- loop: 1 = 5\n- invocations: reduce, COMPONENT_TYPES.includes, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem = 4 = 8\n- bindings: items, breakdown, item, typedItem = 4\n- conditionals: 1 = 4\n- constants: 0,0,0,1 = 4\nTotal: 5+8+4+4+4 = 25\n\nPlus calculateQuotePremium: 23\nTotal: 48\n\nThis is lower than before (56) and removes duplication. But is it clearer? The object spreading is verbose but functional. The intent is \"accumulate a breakdown\". It might be less clear than the for loop with explicit assignments because of the spreads. But the function name reveals intent.\n\nHmm, actually the for loop with assignments might be clearer despite higher mass. But Rule 2 trumps mass. I think for loop is clearer here because we're accumulating totals, and mutate local variables is fine within a pure function.\n\nActually, looking again, the original code with filter+reduce+reduce+filter is quite complex. The single-pass reduce with spreads is also complex. The for loop is probably clearest.\n\nLet me reconsider whether I should do this refactoring. The task says I MUST attempt at least one refactoring. So I need to pick something.\n\nMaybe a simpler, clearer improvement: Add a `Step` union type and clean up `processScenario` casting. That directly improves Rule 2 with minimal mass change.\n\nAlso, refactor `processClaim` to use reduce (or keep for loop but extract `grossPayout`). That's a clear improvement.\n\nAnd maybe rename `isFirstQuote`? Actually, `isFirstQuote` is passed to `calculateFinalPremium` where it's used as `followUpDiscount = isFirstQuote ? 0 : FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE`. So `isFirstQuote` means \"is this the first quote\", which determines whether follow-up discount applies. The name is accurate.\n\nActually, I notice a subtle thing: In `processScenario`, `quoteCount` tracks the number of quotes processed so far. `isFirstQuote = quoteCount === 0`. So the first quote gets no follow-up discount. The second quote gets 15% discount. This matches the test \"should quote a second contract with 15% follow-up discount\". Good. The name `isFirstQuote` is fine.\n\n**Refactoring H: Add type predicates**\n\nInstead of `item as Item` everywhere, add:\n```typescript\nconst asItem = (item: unknown): Item => item as Item;\n```\n\nThis doesn't help much. Or:\n```typescript\nconst itemType = (item: unknown): string => (item as Item).type;\n```\n\nThis could reduce duplication of `(item as Item).type` pattern. Currently used in:\n- standardItemTotals\n- calculateQuotePremium (inside filter)\n- processScenario (insuranceSum reduce)\n\nA helper `itemType(item)` would reveal intent and reduce casts. But `as` is a TypeScript cast, not a runtime invocation. Extracting it to a function adds an invocation each time it's used. Currently each `(item as Item).type` has no invocation (cast is operator). So adding `itemType` helper increases mass (1 invocation per use) but improves clarity.\n\nHmm. Mass increase. Is it worth it? Maybe. It centralizes the casting pattern. But Rule 2 might favor it.\n\nActually, since the types are `unknown[]` for items, the casts are necessary. We could define items as `Item[]` in QuoteStep, but the tests pass objects with only `type` and optional fields. TypeScript would accept `Item[]` for those. Let me check: the test passes `[{ type: \"sword\" }]`. This is assignable to `Item[]` because Item has optional fields. So we could change `QuoteStep` to `items: Item[]` and remove casts.\n\nBut wait, the top-level `ScenarioInput` has `steps: unknown[]`. We cast each step. We could also type steps as `Step[]`. But the test input is a plain JS object, so TypeScript structurally checks it. If we type `steps` as `Step[]`, the test inputs would still satisfy the type because they have the required fields. So we could remove most casts.\n\nLet's see:\n```typescript\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: Item[] };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: unknown };\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\ntype ScenarioInput = { customer: Customer; steps: Step[] };\n```\n\nThen in `processScenario`:\n```typescript\nexport const processScenario = (input: ScenarioInput): { results: StepResult[] } => {\n  ...\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") { ... } // step is QuoteStep in this branch\n    else if (step.op === \"claim\") { ... } // step is ClaimStep\n  }\n}\n```\n\nTypeScript's discriminated union narrowing would work. This eliminates the need for `quoteStep` and `claimStep` casts. We can also remove casts in `quoteStep.items` because it's now `Item[]`.\n\nBut in helper functions like `standardItemTotals`, `calculateQuotePremium`, etc., the items parameter is `unknown[]`. We could change it to `Item[]` too. Then casts inside are unnecessary.\n\nThis is a great Rule 2 improvement! It uses TypeScript's type system to reveal intent instead of runtime casts. It reduces `as` casts (which aren't counted as mass but clutter code).\n\nMass impact: Removing cast expressions doesn't change mass (casts are operators, not components). But we can remove some binding declarations (quoteStep, claimStep) if we use `step` directly. Wait, `step` is already a binding. If we do `if (step.op === \"quote\")`, TypeScript narrows `step` to `QuoteStep`, so we can use `step.items` directly. We don't need `quoteStep` binding. Similarly `claimStep`. So we remove 2 bindings.\n\nAlso, if we change `items: unknown[]` to `items: Item[]` in helper functions, we can remove the `as Item` casts. But those casts don't contribute to mass. However, we might be able to remove the `typedItem` binding in `surchargesForItem` if items are Item[]. Let me see:\n\nCurrently:\n```typescript\nconst surchargesForItem = (item: unknown): number => {\n  const typedItem = item as Item;\n  ...\n};\n```\n\nIf changed to `item: Item`, we can remove `typedItem` and use `item` directly. That removes 1 binding.\n\nSimilarly in `basePremiumForItem`:\n```typescript\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: unknown): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemType((item as Item).type);\n```\nCan become:\n```typescript\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number =>\n  basePremiumForItemType(item.type);\n```\nRemoves cast.\n\nIn `standardItemTotals`:\n```typescript\nitems\n  .filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes((item as Item).type))\n  .reduce(...)\n```\nCan become:\n```typescript\nitems\n  .filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type))\n  .reduce(...)\n```\nNo cast.\n\nIn `calculateQuotePremium`:\n```typescript\nconst count = items.filter((item) => (item as Item).type === type).length;\n```\nCan become:\n```typescript\nconst count = items.filter((item) => item.type === type).length;\n```\nNo cast.\n\nIn `processScenario`:\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = quoteStep.items.reduce(\n  (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType((item as Item).type),\n  0,\n);\n```\nCan become:\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = quoteStep.items.reduce(\n  (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type),\n  0,\n);\n```\nNo cast.\n\nIn `itemPremiumBreakdown` (if I add it):\n```typescript\nconst typedItem = item as Item;\nif (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(typedItem.type)) { ... }\n```\nCan become:\n```typescript\nif (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)) { ... }\n```\n\nSo changing `unknown[]` to `Item[]` improves clarity and reduces bindings. Excellent.\n\nBut wait, the tests pass objects like `{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }`. Are these assignable to `Item`? Yes, because all extra fields are optional or not in Item. Item has `material?: string` so it's fine.\n\nWhat about the discriminated union for steps? The tests pass `{ op: \"quote\", items: [...] }`. This is assignable to `QuoteStep`. Good. `{ op: \"claim\", policy: 0, incident: {...} }` is assignable to `ClaimStep`. Good.\n\nSo this is a safe refactor.\n\n**Refactoring I: Combine changes**\n\nPlan:\n1. Add `Step` type alias as `QuoteStep | ClaimStep`.\n2. Change `ScenarioInput.steps` from `unknown[]` to `Step[]`.\n3. Change helper functions to accept `Item[]` instead of `unknown[]` where appropriate.\n4. Update `processScenario` to use discriminated union narrowing without casting to both step types.\n5. Refactor `processClaim` to use reduce and remove mutable `payout` (optional but good).\n6. Consider single-pass quote premium calculation (maybe too complex).\n\nWait, I should also consider the `processScenario` parameter type. Currently it's `ScenarioInput`. The test calls it with plain objects. If I change `steps: unknown[]` to `steps: Step[]`, TypeScript will still accept the test objects because they structurally match. Good.\n\nBut what about callers outside tests? There are none. Good.\n\nLet me verify by checking if there are other files.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:3","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-24_20-30-02_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_kimi-k2-7-no-thinking && find . -name \"*.ts\" -not -path \"./node_modules/*\" | head -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":261,"output":11721,"cacheRead":10176,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":11634,"totalTokens":22158,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784925841187,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-59f8bdbe-b52b-47cb-a645-167cb804e259","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:3","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"./src/claim-office.spec.ts\n./src/claim-office.ts\n./vitest.config.ts\n./.pi/extensions/subagent/index.ts\n./.pi/extensions/subagent/agents.ts\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784925904121},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, only one implementation file. Let me think more carefully about the refactoring plan.\n\nI need to:\n1. Document naming evaluation\n2. Calculate initial mass\n3. Apply refactorings\n4. Run tests\n5. Calculate final mass\n6. Document decisions\n\nLet me finalize the refactorings.\n\n**Refactoring 1: Use discriminated union types and remove `unknown` casts**\n\nThis addresses Rule 2 (Reveal Intent) by using TypeScript's type system.\n\nChanges:\n- Add `type Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;`\n- Change `ScenarioInput` to `{ customer: Customer; steps: Step[] }`\n- Change `QuoteStep.items` to `Item[]`\n- Change helper parameters from `unknown`/`unknown[]` to `Item`/`Item[]` where appropriate:\n  - `basePremiumForItem(item: Item)`\n  - `surchargesForItem(item: Item)`\n  - `standardItemTotals(items: Item[])` or replace with `itemPremiumBreakdown`\n  - `calculateQuotePremium(items: Item[], ...)`\n- Change `processClaim` incident type? The incident is still `unknown` because it's passed from test. We could create a type:\n  ```typescript\n  type Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\n  type Incident = { damages: Damage[] };\n  ```\n  Then `processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident)`.\n  But the test passes `{ cause: \"fire\", damages: [...] }`. This matches structurally. Good.\n- Remove casts and intermediate variables.\n\n**Refactoring 2: Single-pass quote calculation**\n\nReplace `standardItemTotals` with `itemPremiumBreakdown` that does one pass over items. This addresses Rule 3 (No Duplication) by avoiding multiple iterations.\n\nBut wait, there's another duplication: insuranceSum calculation in processScenario also iterates over items. If I make `itemPremiumBreakdown` also return total insurance value, I could avoid that iteration too. But that mixes quote premium logic with policy cap logic. Maybe not good.\n\nActually, I can add another helper `totalInsuranceValue(items: Item[]): number` that does a single reduce. Then processScenario uses it. But that's just extracting a named helper, not removing duplication.\n\nAlternatively, I can make `processScenario` use the same loop to compute both premium and insurance sum. But `calculateQuotePremium` is a separate function. To avoid that, I'd need to change the architecture.\n\nMaybe simpler: just extract the reduce pattern into a helper `sumBy(items, fn)`. But that's generic and might not belong here.\n\nI think the best Rule 3 improvement is single-pass `itemPremiumBreakdown`. Let me do that.\n\nActually, wait. There's another duplication: the pattern of \"cast to Item and get type\" appears in several places. By changing parameters to `Item`, we remove the casts. This is also Rule 3 (removing duplicated casting pattern).\n\n**Refactoring 3: Simplify processClaim**\n\nChange from mutable `let payout` to functional reduce. This removes duplication of iteration pattern? Actually it just changes style. It reduces assignments and loops? No, it changes for loop to reduce loop. Mass might be similar or slightly lower. Main benefit is removing mutable state, which improves clarity (Rule 2).\n\nLet me carefully recalculate mass for this change.\n\nCurrent processClaim:\n```typescript\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: unknown): ClaimResult => {\n  const typedIncident = incident as { damages: { itemType: string; amount: number }[] };\n  let payout = 0;\n  for (const damage of typedIncident.damages) {\n    payout += damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  payout = Math.min(payout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n\nMass:\n- bindings: policy, incident, typedIncident, payout, damage = 5\n- constants: 0 = 1\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 1 for = 5\n- invocations: Math.min, Math.floor = 2 = 4\n- assignments: payout +=, payout = = 2 = 12\nTotal: 5+1+0+5+4+12 = 27\n\nRefactored with type and reduce:\n```typescript\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\ntype Incident = { damages: Damage[] };\n\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  const grossPayout = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE, 0);\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\n\nMass:\n- bindings: policy, incident, sum, damage, grossPayout, payout = 6\n- constants: 0, 0 = 2\n- conditionals: 0\n- loops: 1 reduce = 5\n- invocations: reduce, Math.min, Math.floor = 3 = 6\n- assignments: 0\nTotal: 6+2+0+5+6+0 = 19\n\nNet change: -8 mass. Good.\n\nBut we add type aliases. Type aliases don't count toward mass (they're type declarations). So this is a mass reduction.\n\nIs this clearer? `grossPayout` reveals intent (before cap and floor). The reduce is clear. I think yes.\n\n**Refactoring 4: Add Step type and simplify processScenario**\n\nCurrent processScenario:\n```typescript\ntype ScenarioInput = { customer: Customer; steps: unknown[] };\n...\nexport const processScenario = (input: ScenarioInput): { results: StepResult[] } => {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: (Policy | undefined)[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    const quoteStep = step as QuoteStep;\n    const claimStep = step as ClaimStep;\n    if (quoteStep.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFirstQuote = quoteCount === 0;\n      results.push({\n        premium: calculateQuotePremium(quoteStep.items, input.customer, isFirstQuote),\n      });\n      const insuranceSum = quoteStep.items.reduce(\n        (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType((item as Item).type),\n        0,\n      );\n      policies.push({ items: quoteStep.items, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER });\n      quoteCount += 1;\n    } else if (claimStep.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies[claimStep.policy] as Policy;\n      const claimResult = processClaim(policy, claimStep.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = claimResult.remainingCap;\n      results.push(claimResult);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nRefactored:\n```typescript\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\ntype ScenarioInput = { customer: Customer; steps: Step[] };\n\nexport const processScenario = (input: ScenarioInput): { results: StepResult[] } => {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFirstQuote = quoteCount === 0;\n      results.push({\n        premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items, input.customer, isFirstQuote),\n      });\n      const insuranceSum = step.items.reduce(\n        (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type),\n        0,\n      );\n      policies.push({ items: step.items, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER });\n      quoteCount += 1;\n    } else if (step.op === \"claim\") {\n      const policy = policies[step.policy];\n      const claimResult = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = claimResult.remainingCap;\n      results.push(claimResult);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nChanges:\n- Added `Step` type\n- `steps: Step[]`\n- Removed `quoteStep` and `claimStep` bindings (2 fewer bindings)\n- Removed casts to `QuoteStep` and `ClaimStep`\n- Removed cast `as Policy` for policy lookup (since policies is `Policy[]` not `(Policy | undefined)[]`)\n- Removed `as Item` cast in insuranceSum reduce\n\nWait, can we change `policies: (Policy | undefined)[]` to `Policy[]`? Yes, because we only push policies in quote branches. The claim branch accesses `policies[step.policy]`. If step.policy is valid, it's a Policy. If invalid, it's undefined, but tests won't have invalid claims. With `(Policy | undefined)[]`, TypeScript would require the cast. With `Policy[]`, TypeScript assumes index access returns Policy. This is fine for tests.\n\nMass change:\n- Removed 2 bindings (quoteStep, claimStep): -2\n- Removed 2 casts (not counted, but cleaner)\n- Changed `policies` type doesn't affect runtime mass\n\nActually the cast `as Policy` isn't counted as mass. But removing it is still a clarity win.\n\nAlso, the `else if` is still one conditional. The `if (step.op === \"quote\")` is 1 conditional, `else if (step.op === \"claim\")` is another conditional (nested if). Wait, `else if` is parsed as `else { if (...) }`. So there are 2 `if` keywords. But in terms of control flow paths, it's still one binary decision. The instructions say count `if` occurrences. So 2 conditionals. Same as before.\n\nActually, we could restructure:\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") { ... }\nelse { // must be claim since Step is discriminated union\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThen it's 1 conditional. But TypeScript might not narrow in the else branch unless we have a type guard. With discriminated union, TypeScript should narrow `step` to `ClaimStep` in the else branch because the only other option is claim. Let me verify. Yes, TypeScript narrows discriminated unions in else branches.\n\nSo we can write:\n```typescript\nif (step.op === \"quote\") {\n  // step is QuoteStep\n} else {\n  // step is ClaimStep\n}\n```\n\nThis reduces conditionals from 2 to 1! But is it clearer? It relies on the type system to ensure exhaustive handling. If we add a new Step type later, TypeScript would catch it. I think it's clearer and removes duplication of checking `op === \"claim\"`.\n\nBut wait, runtime safety: if someone passes an object with op not equal to \"quote\" or \"claim\", the else branch would run. But TypeScript prevents that at compile time. The tests won't do that. This is fine.\n\nMass saved: 1 conditional = 4. Good.\n\nHowever, if the else branch is long, it might be less readable. But here it's not too long.\n\nActually, I think keeping `else if (step.op === \"claim\")` is fine and more explicit. But `else` is cleaner with discriminated union. I'll use `else`.\n\nWait, but the original code used `else if (claimStep.op === \"claim\")`. The cast was needed because `claimStep` was `step as ClaimStep`. With the union, we can do `else if (step.op === \"claim\")`. TypeScript narrows to ClaimStep. So we can keep the explicit check if we want. But using just `else` saves a conditional.\n\nI think for clarity, `else if` is more explicit. The mass cost is small. But the instructions prioritize Rule 2 over mass. Hmm. Actually, `else` with discriminated union is a well-known pattern and is clean. I'll use `else`.\n\n**Refactoring 5: Single-pass premium breakdown**\n\nLet me design this carefully.\n\nCurrent helpers:\n- `standardItemTotals(items)` - filters components, reduces to base and surcharges\n- `calculateQuotePremium` - calls standardItemTotals, then reduces COMPONENT_TYPES and filters items for each\n\nNew helper:\n- `itemPremiumBreakdown(items)` - single reduce/for loop returning standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts\n\nThen `calculateQuotePremium` computes componentBase from componentCounts.\n\nI'll use a for loop for clarity:\n\n```typescript\ntype PremiumBreakdown = {\n  standardBase: number;\n  itemSurcharges: number;\n  componentCounts: Record<string, number>;\n};\n\nconst itemPremiumBreakdown = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown => {\n  let standardBase = 0;\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      standardBase += basePremiumForItem(item);\n      itemSurcharges += surchargesForItem(item);\n    }\n  }\n  return { standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts };\n};\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const { standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts } = itemPremiumBreakdown(items);\n  const componentBase = COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce(\n    (sum, type) => sum + componentPremiumFor(componentCounts[type] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  const policyBase = standardBase + componentBase;\n  return calculateFinalPremium(policyBase, itemSurcharges, customer.yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n};\n```\n\nMass calculation for this part:\n\nitemPremiumBreakdown:\n- bindings: items, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, item = 5\n- constants: 0, 0, {}, 0, 1 = 5 (0, 0 initializers; {} is not a literal value in the mass sense? It's an object literal. Hmm. The instructions say \"Constant: Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`)\". Object literal `{}` is a literal too. I'll count it as 1. Then `0` in `(componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0)` is 1. Then `+ 1` is 1. So constants: 0,0,{},0,1 = 5.\n- conditionals: 1 if = 4\n- loops: 1 for = 5\n- invocations: COMPONENT_TYPES.includes, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem = 3 = 6\n- assignments: componentCounts[...] = ..., standardBase +=, itemSurcharges += = 3 = 18\nTotal: 5+5+4+5+6+18 = 43\n\ncalculateQuotePremium:\n- bindings: items, customer, isFirstQuote, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentCounts, policyBase, sum, type = 9\n- constants: 0 = 1\n- loops: 1 reduce = 5\n- invocations: itemPremiumBreakdown, reduce, componentPremiumFor, calculateFinalPremium = 4 = 8\nTotal: 9+1+5+8 = 23\n\nCombined: 66\n\nBefore:\nstandardItemTotals: 25\ncalculateQuotePremium: 31\nCombined: 56\n\nSo mass increases by 10. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: Use reduce with object spread to avoid assignments:\n\n```typescript\nconst itemPremiumBreakdown = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown =>\n  items.reduce((breakdown, item) => {\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)) {\n      return {\n        ...breakdown,\n        componentCounts: {\n          ...breakdown.componentCounts,\n          [item.type]: (breakdown.componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1,\n        },\n      };\n    }\n    return {\n      ...breakdown,\n      standardBase: breakdown.standardBase + basePremiumForItem(item),\n      itemSurcharges: breakdown.itemSurcharges + surchargesForItem(item),\n    };\n  }, { standardBase: 0, itemSurcharges: 0, componentCounts: {} });\n```\n\nMass:\n- bindings: items, breakdown, item = 3\n- constants: 0, 0, {}, 0, 1 = 5\n- conditionals: 1 = 4\n- loops: 1 reduce = 5\n- invocations: reduce, COMPONENT_TYPES.includes, basePremiumForItem, surchargesForItem = 4 = 8\n- assignments: 0\nTotal: 3+5+4+5+8 = 25\n\nPlus calculateQuotePremium: 23\nTotal: 48\n\nThis is lower than before (56). But is it clearer? The spread operators make it harder to read. Rule 2 might favor the for loop. But Rule 4 (fewest elements) favors fewer mass.\n\nActually, the object spread creates new objects on every iteration, which is inefficient. For this small domain it doesn't matter, but it's not great.\n\nAnother option: Use reduce with mutable accumulator (the breakdown object is mutated):\n\n```typescript\nconst itemPremiumBreakdown = (items: Item[]): PremiumBreakdown =>\n  items.reduce((breakdown, item) => {\n    if (COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)) {\n      breakdown.componentCounts[item.type] = (breakdown.componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      breakdown.standardBase += basePremiumForItem(item);\n      breakdown.itemSurcharges += surchargesForItem(item);\n    }\n    return breakdown;\n  }, { standardBase: 0, itemSurcharges: 0, componentCounts: {} });\n```\n\nMass:\n- bindings: item