nBefore was 56. Still higher by 4.\n\nBut we removed `standardItemTotals` function entirely. That saves the function declaration overhead (no runtime mass though, since function declarations aren't counted as components). Actually function declarations themselves aren't counted. Only their bodies count. So removing `standardItemTotals` removes its body mass.\n\nWait, I already counted `standardItemTotals` and `calculateQuotePremium` separately. If I inline, I remove `standardItemTotals` mass (25) and add its logic to `calculateQuotePremium`. The inlined `calculateQuotePremium` mass is 60. Before combined was 56. So +4.\n\nHmm. The single-pass approach adds assignments which increase mass. The main benefit is conceptual: avoid iterating items 4 times. That's a clarity/performance win, but mass-wise it's worse.\n\nMaybe I shouldn't do single-pass. Instead, focus on the type system improvements and processClaim refactor.\n\n**Alternative Rule 3 improvement: Remove duplicated casting**\n\nBy changing item types from `unknown` to `Item`, I remove duplicated `(item as Item)` casts. This is a Rule 3 win that doesn't increase mass. Good.\n\n**Refactoring decision:**\n\nI'll do these refactorings:\n1. Add `Step` union type and improve `ScenarioInput`.\n2. Change `QuoteStep.items` and helper function parameters to `Item[]` to remove casts.\n3. Refactor `processClaim` to use `Incident` type and reduce (remove mutable state).\n4. Use discriminated union narrowing in `processScenario` with `else` branch.\n\nI won't do the single-pass premium breakdown because it increases mass and the current code is reasonably clear. Or should I? The instructions say I must attempt at least one refactoring. I have multiple. So it's fine to skip single-pass if it doesn't clearly improve.\n\nWait, but \"No Duplication\" - the current code does iterate over items multiple times. Is that duplication? It's duplicated iteration pattern. By using `Item[]` types, I remove the duplicated casts but not the duplicated iteration. Hmm.\n\nMaybe a smaller duplication removal: The insurance sum calculation and component premium calculation both need item types. But they compute different things. Not clear duplication.\n\nActually, there's conceptual duplication: both `standardItemTotals` and the component count logic classify items as components vs standard. They both check `COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)`. If I extract a helper `isComponent(item)`, that centralizes the classification.\n\n```typescript\nconst isComponent = (item: Item): boolean => COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type);\n```\n\nThen use it in `standardItemTotals`:\n```typescript\nitems.filter((item) => !isComponent(item))\n```\n\nAnd in `calculateQuotePremium`:\n```typescript\nconst count = items.filter((item) => isComponent(item) && item.type === type).length;\n```\n\nWait, that changes logic. `COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type)` checks if type is any component. `item.type === type` checks specific type. The original `items.filter((item) => (item as Item).type === type).length` counts items of a specific type. This doesn't use `isComponent`. But since `type` is from `COMPONENT_TYPES`, any item with `item.type === type` is automatically a component. So we could add `isComponent(item)` but it's redundant.\n\nActually, the conceptual duplication is \"classify whether an item is a component\". `standardItemTotals` does this via `!COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(...)`. The component count logic doesn't need to re-check because it iterates known component types. But it still filters by `item.type === type`, which is different.\n\nHmm, not strong duplication.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe I should do the single-pass breakdown despite mass increase, because it genuinely removes duplicated iteration and makes the algorithm clearer. Rule 2 trumps APP.\n\nActually, I realize I might have overcounted assignments. Let me re-check the instructions:\n\"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\"\n\nIs `let payout = 0` an assignment or just a binding? In the example, they don't have let. I think initializer of let/const is part of binding, not assignment. But I'm not 100% sure. If `let payout = 0` counts as an assignment, then the for loop version has more assignments than I counted.\n\nIf `const x = 5` counts as assignment, then mass would be much higher everywhere. But the instructions distinguish \"Binding/Scalar (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters\" and \"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations\". So binding declaration is 1, mutation assignment is 6. I think initializer is part of declaration. So `let payout = 0` is binding only.\n\nBut `payout += damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE` is a compound assignment, which is a mutation. So assignment.\n\nOK, my counts are probably right.\n\nLet me consider another small refactoring: extract `DAMAGE_TYPES`? Not present.\n\nWhat about combining constants? No.\n\nWhat about renaming `processScenario`? It's the main export. Tests import it. Can't rename.\n\nWhat about `calculateFinalPremium` - the name is fine. But the function uses `policyMultiplier` which might be unclear. It's `1 + surcharge - discounts`. The name `policyMultiplier` is okay.\n\nWhat about `basePremiumForItemType` - returns base premium based on type. Fine.\n\n`insuranceValueForItemType` - returns insurance value based on type. Fine.\n\nI think the type system refactorings are the best balance of clarity and mass.\n\nLet me also think about adding a helper for `insuranceSum` to make processScenario clearer:\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = quoteStep.items.reduce(\n  (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType((item as Item).type),\n  0,\n);\n```\n\nRefactored (with Item[] type):\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = step.items.reduce(\n  (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type),\n  0,\n);\n```\n\nThis is clear enough. We could extract:\n```typescript\nconst totalInsuranceValue = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type), 0);\n```\n\nThen in processScenario:\n```typescript\nconst insuranceSum = totalInsuranceValue(step.items);\n```\n\nThis adds a function (mass of the function) but makes processScenario clearer and removes the duplicated reduce pattern. Is it worth it? \n\nMass of new helper:\n- bindings: items, sum, item = 3\n- constants: 0 = 1\n- loops: 1 reduce = 5\n- invocations: reduce, insuranceValueForItemType = 2 = 4\nTotal: 3+1+5+4 = 13\n\nIn processScenario, we remove:\n- binding sum, item = 2\n- constant 0 = 1\n- loop reduce = 5\n- invocations reduce, insuranceValueForItemType = 2 = 4\nTotal removed: 2+1+5+4 = 12\n\nNet: +1 mass. But we add a named helper that reveals intent. Since it's used only once, the benefit is small. I'll skip it.\n\nActually, wait. If I do single-pass breakdown and also return insurance sum, then I don't need this helper. But that's mixing concerns. Let me think...\n\nThe single-pass breakdown computes premium-related values. Insurance value is for policy cap. They are different. Mixing them in one helper would be poor cohesion. Better separate.\n\nOK, I won't do single-pass. I'll focus on type improvements and processClaim.\n\nLet me also consider removing `basePremiumForItem` and `surchargesForItem` parameters being `unknown`. If I change them to `Item`, I remove casts. Good.\n\nNow let me think about the `Item` type. Currently:\n```typescript\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number; material?: string };\n```\n\nIt has `material` which is only used in tests but not in logic. That's fine.\n\nWhen I change `QuoteStep.items` to `Item[]`, tests still pass. Good.\n\nNow let me write the refactored code and verify tests.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider: should I keep `processClaim` with `for...of` or use reduce? The reduce version has lower mass and removes mutable state. I think reduce is a good improvement. But some might argue for loop is more readable. Since we're summing damages, reduce is idiomatic. I'll use reduce.\n\nOne concern: `const grossPayout = incident.damages.reduce((sum, damage) => sum + damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE, 0);` - is operator precedence correct? `sum + damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE` is `(sum + damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE`. Same as original. Good.\n\nLet me also check: `return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };` - payout here is the capped amount (after Math.min). Original code: `payout = Math.min(payout, policy.remainingCap);` then returns `policy.remainingCap - payout`. Same. Good.\n\nNow let me write the full refactored file.\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM = 25;\n\ntype QuoteResult = { premium: number };\ntype ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\ntype StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: Item[] };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: Incident };\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number; material?: string };\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\ntype Policy = { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number };\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\ntype Incident = { damages: Damage[] };\n\nconst basePremiumForItemType = (type: string): number => (type === \"amulet\" ? 60 : 100);\n\nconst insuranceValueForItemType = (type: string): number => {\n  if (type === \"sword\") return 1000;\n  if (type === \"amulet\") return 600;\n  if (type === \"staff\") return 800;\n  if (type === \"potion\") return 400;\n  if (type === \"rune\" || type === \"moonstone\") return 250;\n  return 0;\n};\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number => basePremiumForItemType(item.type);\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\nconst surchargesForItem = (item: Item): number => {\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForItem(item);\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed ? basePremium * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentSurcharge =\n    (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? basePremium * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n\nconst componentPremiumFor = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM;\n\nconst calculateFinalPremium = (\n  policyBase: number,\n  itemSurcharges: number,\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFirstQuote ? 0 : FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  const policyMultiplier =\n    1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n  const beforeFee = policyBase * policyMultiplier + itemSurcharges;\n  const roundedToNearestCent = Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100;\n  return Math.ceil(roundedToNearestCent + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nconst standardItemTotals = (items: Item[]): { base: number; surcharges: number } =>\n  items\n    .filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type))\n    .reduce(\n      (totals, item) => ({\n        base: totals.base + basePremiumForItem(item),\n        surcharges: totals.surcharges + surchargesForItem(item),\n      }),\n      { base: 0, surcharges: 0 },\n    );\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const { base: standardBase, surcharges: itemSurcharges } = standardItemTotals(items);\n  const componentBase = COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce((sum, type) => {\n    const count = items.filter((item) => item.type === type).length;\n    return sum + componentPremiumFor(count);\n  }, 0);\n  const policyBase = standardBase + componentBase;\n  return calculateFinalPremium(policyBase, itemSurcharges, customer.yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n};\n\ntype ScenarioInput = { customer: Customer; steps: Step[] };\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\n\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  const grossPayout = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE,\n    0,\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (input: ScenarioInput): { results: StepResult[] } => {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFirstQuote = quoteCount === 0;\n      results.push({\n        premium: calculateQuotePremium(step.items, input.customer, isFirstQuote),\n      });\n      const insuranceSum = step.items.reduce(\n        (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type),\n        0,\n      );\n      policies.push({ items: step.items, remainingCap: insuranceSum * CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER });\n      quoteCount += 1;\n    } else {\n      const policy = policies[step.policy];\n      const claimResult = processClaim(policy, step.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = claimResult.remainingCap;\n      results.push(claimResult);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n```\n\nWait, I need to check TypeScript narrowing. In the else branch, is `step` narrowed to `ClaimStep`? Yes, because `Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep` and we checked `step.op === \"quote\"`. In the else branch, `op` cannot be \"quote\", so it must be \"claim\", so it's `ClaimStep`.\n\nAlso, `policies: Policy[]` and `policies[step.policy]` returns `Policy` (not `Policy | undefined`). TypeScript treats array index access as returning the element type. Good.\n\nNow let me think about mass calculation after refactoring.\n\nI'll recalculate all components.\n\nTop-level constants:\n- 5, 0.1, 0.5 (3)\n- \"rune\", \"moonstone\" (2)\n- 3, 60, 25 (3)\n- basePremiumForItemType: \"amulet\", 60, 100 (3)\n- insuranceValueForItemType: \"sword\", 1000, \"amulet\", 600, \"staff\", 800, \"potion\", 400, \"rune\", \"moonstone\", 250, 0 (12)\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD: 5 (1)\n- HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE: 0.3 (1)\n- LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE: 0.2 (1)\n- LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD: 2 (1)\n- FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE: 0.15 (1)\n- surchargesForItem: 0, 0, 0 (3)\n- componentPremiumFor: none\n- calculateFinalPremium: 0, 0, 1, 100, 100 (5)\n- standardItemTotals: 0, 0 (2)\n- calculateQuotePremium: 0 (1)\n- DEDUCTIBLE: 100 (1)\n- CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER: 2 (1)\n- processClaim: 0 (1)\n- processScenario: 0, 0, 1 (3)\n\nTotal constants: same as before? Let's see: 3+2+3+3+12+1+1+1+1+1+3+0+5+2+1+1+1+1+3 = 44. Same.\n\nBindings:\nTop-level const bindings: 7 (PROCESSING_FEE, FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE, CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE, COMPONENT_TYPES, COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE, COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM, COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM)\nbasePremiumForItemType: type (1)\ninsuranceValueForItemType: type (1)\nbasePremiumForItem: item (1)\nHIGH_... constants: 5\nsurchargesForItem: item, basePremium, curseSurcharge, enchantmentSurcharge (4) [removed typedItem]\ncomponentPremiumFor: count (1)\ncalculateFinalPremium: policyBase, itemSurcharges, yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote, loyaltyDiscount, followUpDiscount, policyMultiplier, beforeFee, roundedToNearestCent (9)\nstandardItemTotals: items, item, totals (3)\ncalculateQuotePremium: items, customer, isFirstQuote, standardBase, itemSurcharges, componentBase, policyBase, sum, type, count (10)\nDEDUCTIBLE, CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER (2)\nprocessClaim: policy, incident, sum, damage, grossPayout, payout (6