ssScenario: for...of + reduce = 2\n\nTotal loops: 7\n\nAssignments:\n- processScenario: quoteCount += 1, policy.remainingCap = claimResult.remainingCap = 2\n\nTotal assignments: 2\n\nTotal mass = 44*1 + 62*1 + 24*2 + 12*4 + 7*5 + 2*6\n= 44 + 62 + 48 + 48 + 35 + 12\n= 249\n\nOriginal mass: 260\nMass change: -11\n\nLet me verify original mass once more.\n\nOriginal code had:\n- constants: 44\n- bindings: 63\n- invocations: 21\n- conditionals: 13\n- loops: 7\n- assignments: 4\n\nTotal = 44 + 63 + 42 + 52 + 35 + 24 = 260. Correct.\n\nNew total = 249. Correct.\n\nMass change = 260 - 249 = 11 reduction.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether there are additional refactorings I should do. The instructions say I must attempt at least one. I've done several. Tests pass. Mass reduced. Good.\n\nBut I want to make sure I'm applying the rules in priority order:\n1. Tests Pass: Yes, tests pass.\n2. Reveals Intent: Yes, improved type names (Step, Incident, Damage), removed scattered casts, clearer processClaim with grossPayout.\n3. No Duplication: Removed duplicated `as Item` casts by using Item[] types. Removed duplicated step casts by using Step union. Could argue for more duplication removal (multiple iterations) but I chose clarity.\n4. Fewest Elements: Mass reduced. Removed one conditional branch and two mutable assignments.\n\nI should document the naming evaluation.\n\nNaming Evaluation:\n- `processScenario`: clearly processes a customer scenario through its steps. Keep.\n- `calculateQuotePremium`: calculates premium for a quote. Keep.\n- `calculateFinalPremium`: applies policy-wide modifiers and fee. Keep.\n- `basePremiumForItemType`/`basePremiumForItem`: clear. Keep.\n- `insuranceValueForItemType`: clear. Keep.\n- `surchargesForItem`: clear. Keep.\n- `componentPremiumFor`: clear. Keep.\n- `standardItemTotals`: returns base and surcharges for non-component items. Name is okay but slightly ambiguous (does \"standard\" mean non-component?). Keep for now.\n- `processClaim`: 