e to know counts? Not needed.\n\nMaybe we can remove the `standardItemBaseAndSurcharges` rename? It improved naming. Keep.\n\nNow we need produce final report. But before final, perhaps run linter? Not required. But check TypeScript compiles? `pnpm test` did.\n\nLet's read final file to ensure correctness.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.read:16","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/claim-office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":85,"output":4068,"cacheRead":26240,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4048,"totalTokens":30393,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784926250551,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-fdcd28a3-cc01-4dae-bec7-4f1568a69d21","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.read:16","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"const PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.5;\n\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = [\"rune\", \"moonstone\"];\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\nconst COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM = 25;\n\ntype QuoteResult = { premium: number };\ntype ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\ntype StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\ntype Item = { type: string; cursed?: boolean; enchantment?: number; material?: string };\ntype Customer = { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\ntype Incident = { damages: Damage[] };\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items: Item[] };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: Incident };\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\ntype Policy = { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number };\n\nconst basePremiumForItemType = (type: string): number => (type === \"amulet\" ? 60 : 100);\n\nconst INSURANCE_VALUES: Record<string, number> = {\n  sword: 1000,\n  amulet: 600,\n  staff: 800,\n  potion: 400,\n  rune: 250,\n  moonstone: 250,\n};\n\nconst insuranceValueForItemType = (type: string): number =>\n  INSURANCE_VALUES[type] ?? 0;\n\nconst basePremiumForItem = (item: Item): number => basePremiumForItemType(item.type);\n\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE = 0.3;\nconst LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE = 0.15;\n\nconst surchargesForItem = (item: Item): number => {\n  const basePremium = basePremiumForItem(item);\n  const curseSurcharge = item.cursed ? basePremium * CURSE_SURCHARGE_RATE : 0;\n  const enchantmentSurcharge =\n    (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD\n      ? basePremium * HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE\n      : 0;\n  return curseSurcharge + enchantmentSurcharge;\n};\n\nconst componentPremiumFor = (count: number): number =>\n  count === COMPONENT_BLOCK_SIZE ? COMPONENT_BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_UNIT_PREMIUM;\n\nconst policyMultiplierFor = (\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFirstQuote ? 0 : FOLLOW_UP_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n  return 1 + FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_RATE - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount;\n};\n\nconst calculateFinalPremium = (\n  policyBase: number,\n  itemSurcharges: number,\n  yearsWithMHPCO: number,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const policyMultiplier = policyMultiplierFor(yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n  const beforeFee = policyBase * policyMultiplier + itemSurcharges;\n  const roundedToNearestCent = Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100;\n  return Math.ceil(roundedToNearestCent + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n\nconst standardItemBaseAndSurcharges = (items: Item[]): { base: number; surcharges: number } =>\n  items\n    .filter((item) => !COMPONENT_TYPES.includes(item.type))\n    .reduce(\n      (totals, item) => ({\n        base: totals.base + basePremiumForItem(item),\n        surcharges: totals.surcharges + surchargesForItem(item),\n      }),\n      { base: 0, surcharges: 0 },\n    );\n\nconst countByType = <T>(items: T[], getType: (item: T) => string): Record<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    const type = getType(item);\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    return counts;\n  }, {} as Record<string, number>);\n\nconst componentPremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const counts = countByType(items, (item) => item.type);\n  return COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce(\n    (sum, type) => sum + componentPremiumFor(counts[type] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n};\n\nconst calculateQuotePremium = (\n  items: Item[],\n  customer: Customer,\n  isFirstQuote: boolean,\n): number => {\n  const { base: standardBase, surcharges: itemSurcharges } = standardItemBaseAndSurcharges(items);\n  const policyBase = standardBase + componentPremiumForItems(items);\n  return calculateFinalPremium(policyBase, itemSurcharges, customer.yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote);\n};\n\ntype ScenarioInput = { customer: Customer; steps: unknown[] };\n\nconst DEDUCTIBLE = 100;\nconst CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER = 2;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD = 8;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 0.5;\n\nconst reimbursementRateFor = (item: Item): number => {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD)\n    return HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;\n  return 1;\n};\n\nconst damagePayoutFor = (damage: Damage, policy: Policy): number => {\n  const item = policy.items.find((i) => i.type === damage.itemType);\n  const rate = item ? reimbursementRateFor(item) : 1;\n  return damage.amount * rate - DEDUCTIBLE;\n};\n\nconst validateClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): void => {\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Negative damage amount\");\n  }\n  const damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages, (damage) => damage.itemType);\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items, (item) => item.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    if (count > (policyCounts[type] ?? 0))\n      throw new Error(\"Too many damage entries for item type\");\n  }\n};\n\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  validateClaim(policy, incident);\n  const grossPayout = incident.damages.reduce(\n    (sum, damage) => sum + damagePayoutFor(damage, policy),\n    0,\n  );\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n\nexport const processScenario = (input: ScenarioInput): { results: StepResult[] } => {\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies: Policy[] = [];\n  let quoteCount = 0;\n  for (const step of input.steps) {\n    const typedStep = step as Step;\n    if (typedStep.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFirstQuote = quoteCount === 0;\n      results.push({\n        premium: calculateQuotePremium(typedStep.items, input.customer, isFirstQuote),\n      });\n      const insuranceSum = typedStep.items.reduce(\n        (sum, item) => sum + insuranceValueForItemType(item.type),\n        0,\n      );\n      policies.push({\n        items: typedStep.items,\n        remainingCap: insuranceSum * CLAIM_CAP_MULTIPLIER,\n      });\n      quoteCount += 1;\n    } else {\n      const policy = policies[typedStep.policy];\n      const claimResult = processClaim(policy, typedStep.incident);\n      policy.remainingCap = claimResult.remainingCap;\n      results.push(claimResult);\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n};\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784926274511},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The code looks good.\n\nNow we need consider whether to run TypeScript check `tsc --noEmit` to ensure no type errors. The test ran, but maybe `tsx`? Let's run tsc.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"functions.bash:17","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"npx tsc --noEmit","timeout":60}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/kimi-k2.7-code","usage":{"input":1813,"output":73,"cacheRead":30336,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":44,"totalTokens":32222,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784926274511,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-204065b9-5fb5-4ba3-ae33-be425ff475d2","responseModel":"moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"functions.bash:17","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(no output)"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784926276299},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No type errors. Good.\n\nNow final report. Need include:\n- Naming evaluation.\n- Initial APP mass (405).\n- Simple design rules evaluation.\n- Refactorings applied.\n- New APP mass (407).\n- Mass change +2.\n- Tests all passing.\n- Explanation that mass increased slightly but clarity/DRY improved, Rule 2 priority.\n\nNeed also mention that we did mandatory refactoring. Could mention we considered no improvement possible? No, we made improvements.\n\nBut wait, is mass increase acceptable? The guidelines say lower mass generally better; Rule 2 trumps. We can justify. However, maybe we can do one more refactor to actually lower mass to make report nicer. Let's see if there's a low-hanging fruit that reduces mass more than the helper added. For example, in `validateClaim`, we can eliminate the `for-of` loop by integrating negative check into countByType selector, saving a loop (mass 5). That would lower total mass below initial. But it couples validation with counting. Is it clear? Let's evaluate.\n\nOption:\n```\nconst validateClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): void => {\n  const damageCounts = countByType(incident.damages, (damage) => {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) throw new Error(\"Negative damage amount\");\n    return damage.itemType;\n  });\n  const policyCounts = countByType(policy.items, (item) => item.type);\n  for (const [type, count] of Object.entries(damageCounts)) {\n    if (count > (policyCounts[type] ?? 0))\n      throw new Error(\"Too many damage entries for item type\");\n  }\n};\n```\nMass effect: remove for-of loop (-5), remove `damage` binding from for-of (-1). But add conditional inside selector (conditional already counted? It was in for-of; still one conditional). The selector arrow function adds a `return` statement not counted. So net -6 mass. Total would become 401 (from 407). Lower than initial 405 by 4. That would be nice. But does it reduce clarity? It hides validation inside a counting helper. However, the helper is named `countByType`; performing validation there is a side effect, less clear. We could rename helper to `countDamagesByType` or `validateAndCount` but not generic.\n\nMaybe better: create a separate helper `assertNoNegativeAmounts`? That adds elements.\n\nThe for-of negative check is already clear. Combining might violate Rule 2. But we could extract a helper `validateDamageAmounts`? Not mass.\n\nAlternatively, we can reduce mass by removing the generic type parameter and using `unknown`? That removes T binding. But less type safety. Not good.\n\nMaybe we can lower mass by simplifying `componentPremiumForItems` to avoid `counts` variable. For example:\n```\nconst componentPremiumForItems = (items: Item[]): number =>\n  COMPONENT_TYPES.reduce(\n    (sum, type) => sum + componentPremiumFor(countByType(items, (item) => item.type)[type] ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n```\nThis removes `counts` binding (-1) but calls countByType inside reduce (multiple times) which is inefficient and adds invocation per iteration. Bad.\n\nMaybe we can reduce mass by not using countByType in validateClaim and instead using `policy.items.filter` but with a helper count for damages. That would lower mass because we remove one countByType call and helper? Wait helper still needed for componentPremiumForItems. But if helper only used once, DRY benefit gone. So no.\n\nMaybe we can find a mass reduction elsewhere independent. For example, `calculateFinalPremium` has intermediate variables. Could inline `policyMultiplier` and `roundedToNearestCent`:\n```\nconst calculateFinalPremium = (...): number => {\n  const beforeFee = policyBase * policyMultiplierFor(yearsWithMHPCO, isFirstQuote) + itemSurcharges;\n  return Math.ceil(Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100 + PROCESSING_FEE);\n};\n```\nMass change: remove `policyMultiplier` binding (-1), remove `policyMultiplierFor` invocation? Actually invocation still present (inline). Wait remove `const policyMultiplier = policyMultiplierFor(...)` and use `policyMultiplierFor(...)` directly. Invocation count unchanged. Remove `roundedToNearestCent` binding (-1) and `beforeFee` still there. But `Math.round(...)` inline. Remove one binding. Also remove `const roundedToNearestCent` assignment? It's a variable declaration not assignment. So mass -2. This is a clarity trade-off: intermediate variable explains rounding; inlining may be less clear. But `Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100` is standard. Could be okay.\n\nSimilarly, `policyMultiplierFor` could be inlined? Not.\n\n`processClaim` has `const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);` then returns `Math.floor(payout)` and `policy.remainingCap - payout`. Could combine:\n```\nconst processClaim = (policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult => {\n  validateClaim(policy, incident);\n  const grossPayout = incident.damages.reduce(...);\n  const payout = Math.min(grossPayout, policy.remainingCap);\n  return { payout: Math.floor(payout), remainingCap: policy.remainingCap - payout };\n};\n```\nFine.\n\nMaybe `standardItemBaseAndSurcharges` rename improved. Could also rename `totals` to `acc`? Not needed.\n\nWe can do an additional small refactoring: inline `policyMultiplier` and `roundedToNearestCent` in `calculateFinalPremium`. That reduces mass by 2, making final mass 405 (same as initial). But is it clearer? `calculateFinalPremium` currently has clear steps: compute multiplier, compute before fee, round to nearest cent, ceil. Inlining the rounding might reduce clarity. However the function name reveals final premium. The intermediate `roundedToNearestCent` is maybe unnecessary; `Math.round(beforeFee * 100) / 100` is idiomatic. Inlining `policyMultiplier` might make the line longer: `policyBase * policyMultiplierFor(...) + itemSurcharges`. Still clear.\n\nBut we should be careful not to refactor multiple things at once. We already did naming and DRY. We can do one more small simplification to lower mass. But is it necessary? The report can show +2 mass with explanation. However the APP guidelines say lower mass = better code generally. A slight increase due to DRY helper is acceptable. But maybe we can reduce the helper's mass by making it not generic? Let's think.\n\nIf we replace generic helper with a simple function `countItemsByType` and use it for both items and damages by mapping damages? Not good.\n\nWhat if we use a generic helper with `typeKey: keyof T` instead of selector function? That would remove the selector arrow functions and callback params, lowering bindings/invocations. For example:\n```\nconst countByType = <T extends { type?: string; itemType?: string }>(items: T[], key: keyof T): Record<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    const type = String(item[key]);\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    return counts;\n  }, {} as Record<string, number>);\n```\nUsage: `countByType(policy.items, \"type\")`, `countByType(incident.damages, \"itemType\")`. This removes selector arrow functions (2 bindings each, 2 invocations each?) and `getType` parameter. But adds `key` parameter, and `String(...)` call. Mass maybe lower by a few. Type constraint could be `T extends Record<string, unknown>` and `key: keyof T`.\n\nLet's compute approximate. Current helper:\n```\nconst countByType = <T>(items: T[], getType: (item: T) => string): Record<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    const type = getType(item);\n    counts[type] = (counts[type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    return counts;\n  }, {} as Record<string, number>);\n```\nBindings: countByType (1), T (1), items (1), getType (1), counts (1), item (1), type (1) = 7.\nInvocations: reduce (call) + getType(item) (call) + `??` (binary) + `+` (binary) = 4. Also `counts[type]` not invocation. So invocations 4.\nConditionals: 0.\nLoops: reduce (1).\nAssignments: `counts[type] = ...` (1).\n\nMass helper = 7 + 4*2 + 5 + 6 = 26.\n\nAlternative key-based helper:\n```\nconst countByType = <T>(items: T[], key: keyof T): Record<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    counts[String(item[key])] = (counts[String(item[key])] ?? 0) + 1;\n    return counts;\n  }, {} as Record<string, number>);\n```\nBindings: countByType(1), T(1), items(1), key(1), counts(1), item(1) = 6 (no type local, no getType).\nInvocations: reduce(1) + String(1) + `??`(1) + `+`(1) + property access item[key] not invocation. Also `counts[String(item[key])]` property access not invocation. So 4 invocations. But String call occurs twice (two occurrences). So invocations = reduce(1)+String(2)+??(1)++(1)=5.\nLoops: 1.\nAssignments: 1.\nMass = 6 + 5*2 + 5 + 6 = 27. Slightly higher. But usages simpler: `countByType(items, \"type\")` vs `countByType(items, (item) => item.type)`. Usage bindings: no arrow callback params. In current usage, each call has an arrow function with 1 param and 1 property access and a return. The arrow function parameter is a binding, property access not invocation. So usage adds 2 bindings per call (the arrow param and maybe implicit? Actually the arrow function expression itself not binding; `getType` param in helper receives the arrow function reference; the arrow function has parameter `item` (binding) and body `item.type` or `damage.itemType` (property access). It doesn't add local variables. So each call adds 1 binding. With key-based, each call adds 0 bindings (just a string constant). But string constant mass 1 each. So per call usage mass: selector version: 1 binding =1, no constant. key version: 1 constant =1. Same. However selector version has property access inside arrow not counted. key version property access in helper. Hmm.\n\nOverall not big difference.\n\nWhat if we use non-generic with string key and accept `Record<string, unknown>`:\n```\nconst countByType = (items: Record<string, unknown>[], key: string): Record<string, number> =>\n  items.reduce((counts, item) => {\n    counts[String(item[key])] = (counts[String(item[key])] ?? 0) + 1;\n    return counts;\n  }, {} as Record<string, number>);\n```\nRemoves generic T binding. Mass lower by 1. Usage: `countByType(policy.items, \"type\")` but `policy.items` is `Item[]`, compatible with `Record<string, unknown>[]`? TypeScript may require `Item extends Record<string, unknown>` (it does). `Damage` also. So it works. This lowers mass by 1. But type safety slightly less. Still acceptable.\n\nBut maybe the generic selector version is more idiomatic and clear. Let's not over-optimize mass.\n\nCould we instead remove the helper and reduce duplication by reusing `componentPremiumForItems` logic? Not.\n\nLet's consider a different refactoring that clearly lowers mass: Replace the `for-of` negative amount loop in `validateClaim` with a `some` call. That would remove the for-of loop and `damage` binding, but add a `some` loop + callback. Mass similar? Current for-of: loop 5, binding damage 1, conditional if 4. Total 10. Using `some`:\n```\nif (incident.damages.some((damage) => damage.amount < 0)) throw new Error(...);\n```\nLoop: some is iteration (5). Invocation: some call (1) + `<` binary (1) = 2 invocations mass 4. Binding: damage (1). Conditional: if (4). Total = 5 + 4 + 1 + 4 = 14. Higher. Not good.\n\nUsing `find`:\n```\nif (incident.damages.find((damage) => damage.amount